{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Give ear, O heavens, and I will speak; and let the earth hear the words of my mouth.</b> Rashi explained: why did He call heaven and earth as witnesses against them? For if Israel should say, 'We did not accept the covenant upon ourselves,' who comes and belies them? Therefore He called heaven and earth as witnesses against them — witnesses that endure forever — that if they merit, etc. In the versions that read 'that endure forever, that if they merit, etc.,' if so it is all one reason. But in the versions that read 'and furthermore, that if they merit,' if so it is two reasons. And the question arises according to the first reason: do heaven and earth then have a mouth to testify? And one cannot answer that certainly they have no speech and words, except that through the fact that the heavens give dew and rain and the earth gives its yield when one does the will of the Omnipresent, and they do not give when one does not do His will — this is proof that Israel accepted the covenant. If so, what is the meaning of 'and furthermore, that if they merit, etc.'? That is the very same thing as the first reason." "And it appears fitting to settle this version, for our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Shabbat 88a): 'the sixth day' — the extra ה serves to say that all of creation hung and stood suspended until the sixth day, which is the day of the sixth of Sivan; if Israel accept the Torah, well and good, and if not, the world returns to chaos and void. And if so, since we see to this day that heaven and earth endure and were not returned to chaos and void, this is decisive proof that Israel accepted the Torah. And this is what it says: therefore He called heaven and earth as witnesses against them — and what is the testimony they testify? That they endure forever, for their very endurance gives faithful testimony of HaShem that Israel already accepted the Torah, for were it not so, they would not endure and stand, but would already have returned to chaos and void." "And the reason why the endurance of heaven and earth depends on the Torah is that the upper ones and the lower ones are two opposites, and they require an intermediary that joins them together — and that is man, who is composed of matter and form, and this composition is through the Torah. For without the Torah, man would be likened to the beast and would have no portion from the upper ones. Therefore one who engages in Torah makes peace between the host on high and the host below, so that they are not opposed one to the other, for the intermediary joins them. This is what it says: ״יַעֲרֹף כַּמָּטָר לִקְחִי״, for every flow is a joining, and just as the rain that descends from the heavens to the earth is as though it joins heaven to earth — for every flow resembles a joining — so too ״לִקְחִי״, that is, the Torah acquired from the heavens to the earth, is the place where earth and firmament kiss." "<b>Give ear, O heavens, and I will speak.</b> We have already written above, in parashat Vayelech (Deuteronomy 31:7), that Moses said to Joshua: ״כִּי אַתָּה תָּבוֹא אֶת הָעָם״ — the elders of the generation are with you; everything according to their understanding and counsel. But the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Joshua: ״כִּי אַתָּה תָּבִיא״ — against their will, etc. And these do not contradict each other, for Moses is speaking of the lesser ones, whom he called by the name 'the people' — those who kick against every difficult matter and do not accept authority and discipline. Therefore one must speak with them gently and softly, according to their understanding and counsel. But the Holy One, blessed be He, is speaking of the better ones among them, for thus the language implies: ״כִּי אַתָּה תָּבִיא אֶת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — and He did not call them by the name 'people,' for of the great ones of Israel He is speaking, those who accept authority and discipline. Therefore He said: ״כִּי אַתָּה תָּבִיא״ — against their will — and with them one may speak every hard word. And there we demonstrated all of this with clear proofs; see there." "Therefore it says ״הַאֲזִינוּ הַשָּׁמַיִם״ — these are the ones exalted in station, as the height of the heavens is above the earth — ״וַאֲדַבֵּרָה״, for every hard word, as it is written (Genesis 42:30): ״דִּבֶּר הָאִישׁ אֲדֹנֵי הָאָרֶץ אִתָּנוּ קָשׁוֹת״. For those exalted in station accept the discipline of hard speech; but as for the lesser ones, who are likened to the earth, He said: ״וְתִשְׁמַע הָאָרֶץ אִמְרֵי פִי״ — for with them I must speak with soft utterance." "<b>Give ear, O heavens, and I will speak.</b> These are the things I took from the mouth of the Gevurah, for the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Joshua: ״כִּי אַתָּה תָּבִיא אֶת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, against their will — take a staff, etc. And they are harsh things; from Him, blessed be He, I took and learned to speak harshly. But not with every person, for harsh words resemble from every side the rain that falls with force — but not everyone rejoices in it. So too the harsh words that I took and learned from the mouth of the Gevurah to speak in — not everyone rejoices in them, for only men of distinction rejoice in them, but not the majority of the common people. Therefore the rebuker must diminish them, for the honor of Israel, a little here a little there, like the rain that falls drop by drop — therefore it says ״יַעֲרֹף״, which is the language of a drop. However, ״תִּזַּל כַּטַּל אִמְרָתִי״ — what I said of my own to Joshua: ״כִּי אַתָּה תָּבוֹא אֶת הָעָם״ — is according to their understanding and counsel, for this is a matter in which everyone rejoices, like this dew in which everyone rejoices. Therefore I do not need to diminish those words. Therefore he said ״תִּזַּל״, which indicates an abundance of flowing." "And with this, what he mentioned regarding rain ״יַעֲרֹף״, and regarding dew ״תִּזַּל״, is resolved — for the way of the world is the opposite: rain flows and dew drips. Rather, it is to indicate the matter we have mentioned, that all of this is a parable and a path for his conduct with those who hear his words. And what he said, ״לִקְחִי״ — that is, the harsh speech he mentioned, which is likened as though he took it with an arm, with a strong hand, and compelled its acceptance. And what he said, ״אִמְרָתִי״ — that is, a soft utterance, as stated." "<b>As showers upon the grass.</b> Rabbeinu Bachye explained ״שְׂעִירִים״ as like ״צְעִירִים״ (young/tender), and it is the fine rain resembling dew, and it is fitting ״עֲלֵי דֶשֶׁא״, which is a general term for the clothing of the earth in grasses called דֶּשֶׁא, and it alludes to the entirety of the common people, with whom he needs to speak in soft words, unfelt — like this fine rain that does not act forcefully. ״וְכִרְבִיבִים״ — that is the heavy, great rain that falls like an arrow-shot, like רָבִיב וּכְחֵץ — it is fitting ״עֲלֵי עֵשֶׂב״, for each individual grass is called ״עֵשֶׂב״, alluding to the singular chosen ones of the generation who do not enter into the mix with all the common people, and they are intimates who listen attentively to harsh words that fall like an arrow-shot." "And close to this interpretation, Mahari”a wrote in Merkevet ha-Mishneh, that the heavens are a designation for males and the earth for females — ״כִּי כַאֲשֶׁר יֵרֵד הַגֶּשֶׁם וְהַשֶּׁלֶג מִן הַשָּׁמַיִם וְגוֹ׳ וְהוֹלִידָהּ וְהִצְמִיחָהּ״ (Isaiah 55:10) — and it is written at the giving of the Torah (Exodus 19:3): ״כֹּה תֹאמַר לְבֵית יַעֲקֹב״ — these are the women; תֹּאמַר to them is a soft utterance; ״וְתַגֵּיד לִבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — these are the males; תַּגִּיד to them is harsh words, like sinews (גִּידִין). Therefore it says ״הַאֲזִינוּ הַשָּׁמַיִם וַאֲדַבֵּרָה״ — harsh speech with the males; ״וְתִשְׁמַע הָאָרֶץ אִמְרֵי פִי״ — a soft utterance to the females." "And it appears proper to explain the reverse of this, for ״הַשָּׁמַיִם״ — that is, the coarse of spirit, whose coarse disposition is upon them, and with them he spoke harshly. However, the righteous humble themselves down to the earth, and with them he spoke softly, along the lines of what is said (Proverbs 17:10): ״תֵּחַת גְּעָרָה בְּמֵבִין מֵהַכּוֹת כְּסִיל מֵאָה״." "And some say that he said 'Give ear, O heavens' — that they would stand still and cease their motion. And if you would say: during all that time, through what would the world endure? — of this he said: 'My teaching shall drop as the rain,' that the world would endure in the merit of the Torah, which stands in place of rain and dew — 'for man does not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of HaShem does man live' (Deuteronomy 8:3). And perhaps it was of this that he said: 'For I will proclaim the name of HaShem, ascribe greatness to our El' — for when I call upon the name of HaShem to decree some thing contrary to the way of the world, such as 'Sun, in Gibeon, stand still' (Joshua 10:12), and such as 'Let there be dew on the fleece and drought on the earth' (Judges 6:37), and the like, then 'ascribe greatness to our El,' and you shall hearken to the words of the one who decrees upon you in the name of HaShem, in the manner that you did now regarding the decree of 'Give ear, O heavens' — that is, the cessation from their motion." "And as for what is written 'Give ear, O heavens' in the language of command, while 'and let the earth hear' is not in the language of command but rather implies that it would hear of itself — it appears that this is its explanation: For since he called upon heaven and earth to testify concerning them, that if Israel would not hearken then the heavens would not give rain and the earth would not give its yield, therefore he spoke by way of command to the heavens, to say: I command you that if Israel do not keep the Torah, then you shall not give them rain. But as for the earth, I have no need to command it, for if the heavens do not give rain, how would the earth give its yield without rain? Therefore he said: 'and let the earth hear the words of my mouth' — that it would surely hear of itself, without a decree, that it would not give its yield, for this follows from that."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {2 "The final Aramaic phrase נָשְׁקֵי אַרְעָא וּרְקִיעַ ('earth and firmament kiss') echoes the Talmudic idiom for the point of contact between heaven and earth; translated literally to preserve the image.", 8 "Mahari”a identified as an abbreviation, resolved here as the author of Merkevet ha-Mishneh, likely R. Isaac Aboab.", 10 "The speaker referenced throughout is Moses; the masculine singular verbs (אָמַר, אֶקְרָא) refer to him. The Kli Yakar is explaining how Moses could command the heavens to stop their motion."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:16:14.582289383Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}